   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 537 of the laws of 2008, amending the education law\nrelating to a restricted dental faculty license, in relation to extending the\nexpiration of such chapter",
    "basePrintNo": "S7594",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7594-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap 537 of 2008",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7594\n\nSPONSOR: GOLDEN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 537 of the laws of 2008,\namending the education law relating to a restricted dental faculty\nlicense, in relation to extending the expiration of such chapter\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to extend the current provisions of\nlaw concerning licensure of foreign trained dentists who are employed as\nfull-time faculty members at a New York State dental school.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the effective date of Chapter 537 of the Laws of\n2008, which currently sunsets on February 1, 2019. Chapter 537 added\nsection 6604-b to the Education Law to authorize the State Education\nDepartment (SED) to issue a full-time faculty license to foreign-trained\ndentists who are full-time faculty members employed at an approved New\nYork state school of dentistry. In order to be eligible, a foreign-\ntrained dentist must meet the following requirements:\n\n* The dentist must be serving full-time on the faculty of one of New\nYork state's six academic dental centers (Columbia University College of\nDental Medicine, New York University College of Dentistry, University at\nBuffalo School of Dental Medicine, State University of New York at Stony\nBrook School of Dental Medicine, Eastman Institute for Oral Health at\nthe University of Rochester, or Touro College of Dental Medicine).\n\n* The dentist must be a full-time faculty member, which means the indi-\nvidual must devote at least 4 days per week to teaching, patient care,\nresearch or administration.\n\n*The dentist may only practice within the facilities or clinics of the\ndental school or at a clinic or facility that has an affiliation agree-\nment with the school.\n\n*The restricted license is valid for a period of 3 years, the same peri-\nod that a regular license is valid.\n\n*In order to qualify for the license an individual must have at least 6\nyears of pre-professional and professional education which includes the\nsame course work required of a dentist licensed through the regular\npathway.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDental schools in the United States suffer from an ongoing shortage of\nfaculty members. As of 2016, there were 271 open full-time dental facul-\nty positions nationwide. Of those vacant positions, at least 76 were\nvacant for 7-12 months. New York State shoulders a disproportionate\nshare of total U.S. vacancies, with more than 60 unfilled dental faculty\nvacancies at present at the state's six schools.\n\nThe reasons for the relatively high number of vacancies are varied and\ncomplex, and include such factors as a candidate's ability to meet the\nrigorous requirements of faculty positions, time and resource\nconstraints on scholarly pursuits, and the overall number of faculty\nmembers who are reaching the age of retirement that need to be replaced\nin order to maintain the current level of instruction and care provided.\nPerhaps most importantly, according to the annual ADEA Survey of Vacant\nBudgeted Faculty positions, salary considerations have consistently\nranked among the top reasons that faculty positions remain unfilled. The\naverage annual net income in 2014 was $174,780 for private general prac-\ntitioner dentist and $322,200 for a specialist. Compared to an average\n2014 salary from an academic dental school of $93,667, this creates a\npay gap that is difficult to bridge.\n\nThe shortage of dental faculty negatively impacts the state's ability to\ntrain new dentists, and thereby compromises access to dental care.  Such\na lack of access results in diminished health outcomes, which in turn\ndrives unnecessary costs to the State's healthcare system.\n\nSince 2008, the State has addressed the shortage of dental faculty by\nallowing the limited licensure of foreign-trained dentists who serve in\nacademia. The Restricted Dental Faculty Licensure statute has been\nimmensely helpful in ensuring the state's academic dental centers are\nable to attract the talent necessary to provide high quality dental\neducation. There are currently approximately 30 dental faculty members\nusing a restricted license throughout NYS's six academic dental centers,\nand three others awaiting approval for the license.\n\nThis bill would make the Restricted Dental Faculty Licensure statute\npermanent, in order to ensure that this important avenue for addressing\nthe dental faculty shortage remains available to New York State dental\nschools. It would continue all the current limitations applicable to\nforeign-trained dentists practicing under such licenses. Currently,\nevery other state that has a dental school grants foreign trained dental\nfaculty the full privilege of licensure while employed within the\nacademic setting.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nPRIOR EXTENSIONS:\n\n2016: S.6552-A(Golden)/A.9129-A(Glick) - Chapter 140\n2014: S.7183-A(Golden)/A.8660-A(Glick) - Chapter 172\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
